base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{log}_{6}⁡x-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+8{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}-\\frac{5}{x}+8{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}-\\frac{5}{x}+8{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡6}-\\frac{5}{1}+8{e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}-5+8e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481477 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481484 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function  $y=2{log}_{12}⁡x-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+7{e}^{x},$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a17970fe-3001-5d61-8d91-323cad97da24" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481484 #edb/ref 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{log}_{12}⁡x-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}-\\frac{7}{x}+7{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}-\\frac{7}{x}+7{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡12}-\\frac{7}{1}+7{e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡12}-7+7e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481488 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186096615.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481488 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "7814286e-50fe-5b83-8ac5-e2dfc5a44f32" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481491 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481489 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=-\\frac{12}{7}ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2aa37d05-2721-560e-879d-45257a2942c1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481489 #edb/ref 1074 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{12}{7}ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481493 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186096613.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186481493 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "f40367dc-e4a3-50ce-abc9-8a12118b2790" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
